Of particular interest is also the structure of the solution \eref{Lag}. In fact, for any given $k>0$, the horizontal particle path given by \eref{Lag} coupled with \eref{38}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{44}
   \fl x(t;a,b,z)+iy(t;a,b,z)=(a+ib)+\left[d(0)e^{(1+i)\lambda z}+\bi{U}_G\right]t+\\
   +\frac{1}{k}e^{k(b+z)}e^{i\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+k(a-z)-(f+2 k^2)t\right)},\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
is a solution of \eref{governing enqs} and \eref{1cont} satisfying the boundary conditions and representing parametric trochoids. The mean ageostrophic current, over a period $T=\frac{2\pi}{ck}=\frac{2\pi}{f+2 k^2}$,
\begin{eqnarray}\label{45}
\langle \widehat{u}+i\widehat{v}\rangle_T=\frac{1}{T}\int_0^T\left( \widehat{u}(t;a,b,z)+i\widehat{v}(t;a,b,z)\right) dt=d(0)e^{(1+i)\lambda z}
	\end{eqnarray}
is the classical Ekman spiral (see \Fref{Ekman 3d}), while the oscillatory perturbation of the current 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{k}e^{k(b+z)}e^{i\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+k(a-z)-(f+2 k^2)t\right)}
\end{equation}
is near-inertial, as  $ f\approx 15$ and $k<<1$ gives
\begin{equation}
f+2 k^2\approx f
\end{equation}
for the frequency, hence the period of oscillations is approximately the inertial period of the Earth. \\
Averaging over the depth the expression in \eref{45} gives the depth-averaged mean-drift Ekman current of the solution \eref{44}, also known as (time-averaged) Ekman trasport,
\begin{equation}\label{48}
\mathcal{I}_{Ek}:=\int_{-\infty}^0 d(0))e^{(1+i)\lambda z}\, dz=\frac{d(0)}{\lambda \sqrt{2}}e^{-i\frac{\pi}{4}},
\end{equation}
which is at $45^{\circ}$ to the right of the mean surface current $d(0)$; this is a classical result of Ekman's theory.



\section{Discussion}
The presented Lagrangian analysis provides an explicit solution for the nonlinear governing equations - at leading order - for the wind-driven flows in regions away from the poles (where classical spherical coordinates fails, and the use of rotated spherical coordinates is necessary - see Constantin \& Johnson 2023 and Puntini 2025) and the equatorial band (due to the breakdown of the Ekman flow - see Constantin \& Johnson 2015). The solution is the sum of a mean wind-drift current (the well-known Ekman spiral), a background geostrophic current balancing the pressure gradient, and near-inertial oscillations. For every fixed value of $z$, the particle path described \eref{44} represents a trochoid, the only known solution for the $2D$ incompressible Euler equations with free boundary discovered by Gerstner (see e.g. Milne-Thomson 1938, Henry 2008, Constantin \& Strauss 2010 or Abrashkin and Pelinovsky 2022). This study extends the solutions of Constantin 2022a, 2022b and Puntini 2025 for the Arctic Ocean, where ice and the Transpolar Drift Current are present, to mid-latitudes regions. \\
Recalling the momentum equations at leading order
\begin{eqnarray}
		\frac{D u}{D t}  - fv= -\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} +  \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial z^2},\\
		\frac{D v}{D t}+fu= -\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} +  \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial z^2},
	\end{eqnarray}
we can think the solution \eref{44} as a nonlinear ``superposition'' of two terms, namely the Ekman spiral, solution of
\begin{eqnarray}
		 - fv=   \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial z^2},\\
		fu=  \frac{\partial^2 v}{\partial z^2},
	\end{eqnarray}
and the Gerstner's trocoidal solution of the Euler equations 
\begin{eqnarray}
		\frac{D u}{D t}  = -\frac{\partial P}{\partial x},\\
		\frac{D v}{D t}= -\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} .
	\end{eqnarray}
    We also have shown that, knowing the velocity of the wind and of the background geostrophic current, the wind-drift surface current is uniquely determined by the boundary conditions. Moreover, the deflection angle between the wind-drift flow at the surface and the blowing wind (relatively to the geostrophic flow) is less than $45^{\circ}$.\\
In this sense, we also point out a the importance of the boundary conditions. In some textbooks or papers (see e.g. Trenberth 1990 or Marshall \& Plumb 2016) the stress of the wind over the ocean is expresses as 
\begin{equation}\label{stress4}
    \bi{\tau}'_a=\rho'_a C_{aw} |\bu'_a|\bu'_a,
\end{equation}
without taking into account the surface motion of the ocean. Assuming \eref{stress4}, equation \eref{stressADIM} would be
\begin{equation}
    \left(\frac{\p u}{\p z},\, \frac{\p v}{\p z}\right) =\gamma_{aw} C_{aw} |(u_a,v_a)|\,(u_a,\,v_a),
\end{equation}
which, due to  \eref{meancurrent2}, reads as
\begin{equation}\label{4021}
\frac{\lambda  }{C_{aw}\gamma_{aw}} \left(\df_1-\df_2, \df_1+\df_2\right)= |(u_a,v_a)|\,(u_a,v_a),
\end{equation}
or equivalently as
\begin{equation}\label{drift 5}
(\df_1+i\df_2)=e^{-i\frac{\pi}{4}}\beta\sqrt{u^2_a+v^2_a}\,(u_a+iv_a),
\end{equation}
where we have written again $\df_1=d_1(0)$, $\df_2=d_2(0)$ and $\beta=\frac{C_{aw}\gamma_{aw}} {\sqrt{2} \lambda } $. In this case, the deflection angle would be of exactly $45^{\circ}.$\\
We have assumed the vertical eddy viscosity to be constant, which is, to a first approximation, a good choice for oceanic flows; however, a direction that is of interest is to extend this model to the case of depth-dependent or time-dependent eddy viscosities, expanding the recent investigations by Bressan \& Constantin 2019, Constantin \etal 2020, Dritschel \etal 2020, Constantin 2021, Roberti 2021 and Roberti 2022).
