\subsection{Evolutionary epidemic model}\label{subsec2.1}

Modeling the interplay between contagion, immune response, and mutation dynamics presents a significant challenge, as these processes operate at distinct yet compatible spatial and temporal scales. Particularly, contagion arises from interactions between individuals in a population, immune responses occur within the host through virus-antibody interactions, and mutation is governed by the internal dynamics of viral populations. 


Here, we introduce the Susceptible - Infectious - Mutation - Susceptible (SIMS) model, a compartmental framework that integrates all three processes into a unified approach. In the SIMS model, each individual belongs to either the Susceptible (S) compartment or one of $n$ distinct Infectious (I$_i$) compartments, where each I$_i$ represents an infection with strain $s_i \in \mathcal{N}$. Our model considers that intra-host viral dynamics occur on a much faster timescale than epidemiological spread. Consequently, the model neglects intra-host viral diversity and assumes that the infectious state of each individual is defined by a single strain.

\medskip

%The structure of the SIMS model is illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:1}(a) and is detailed as follows. Individuals designated as Susceptible (S) are those who are currently healthy and can get infected through a direct contact with Infectious (I$_i$) agents, being $\lambda$ the associated infectivity. Upon infection, a Susceptible (S) agent transforms into the Infectious (I$_i$) state, where the individual is contagious. Notably, when contagion occurs, Susceptible (S) individuals acquire the viral genotype $s_i$ of the Infected (I$_i$) individual that has infected them. Individuals in this Infectious compartment (I$_i$) remain, on average, $\mu_i^{-1}$ days before recovering and becoming Susceptible again. To capture the \textbf{immune response} of the individuals, we assume that the recovery rate ($\mu_i$) depends on the historic pathogen load of the individual. Specifically, we assume that it starts from a basal recovery rate ($\mu_0$), and increases proportionally to that pathogen load of genotype $s_i$, mediated by the immunity acquisition rate $\alpha$ and the sensibility of the immune system to the pathogen load, captured by the rate $\varsigma$. Moreover, the acquired immunity wanes over time, according to the characteristic waning time, $\gamma^{-1}$. Furthermore, we consider that individuals also acquire partial cross-immunity to other viral genotypes, according to the ratio between the genetic difference among genotypes and the characteristic length of immunity transcendence $\Delta$. Finally, \textbf{mutation} dynamics are captured as a diffusion process through the genotype network. Specifically, each Infected individual is associated to a strain $s_i\in \mathcal{N}$, and can mutate towards any of the neighboring strains $s_j$ with rate $D_x$.  All in all, the SIMS model comprises ($N+1$) compartments and six epidemiological parameters.


