The accuracy of the FT can be significantly better for the well-behaved functions. As~an example, consider a function
\begin{equation}
\label{WBF} % Well-behaved function
g(t) = e^{-(6\pi t)^2} - \sin(32t)e^{-\left(7\pi t\right)^2}.
\end{equation}
The first term of this function is even
\begin{equation}
\label{ECGF} % even component of g-function
g_{even}(t) = \frac{g(t) + g(-t)}{2} = e^{-(6\pi t)^2}
\end{equation}
while its second term is odd
\begin{equation}
\label{OCGF} % odd component of g-function
g_{odd}(t) = \frac{g(t) - g(-t)}{2} = -\sin(32t)e^{-(7\pi t)^2}.
\end{equation}

Figure 5 shows the functions $g(t)$, $g_{even}(t)$ and $g_{odd}(t)$ by blue, red, and green colors, respectively.

