\end{figure}

Hilbert demonstrates that this product is commutative, associative, and distributive. The proofs are based on Pascal's theorem. Figure \ref{hilbert1} (right) illustrates the proof of the commutativity law: Since triangles $\triangle 1O1$ and $\triangle (ab)O(ba)$ are 
isosceles, the equality $ab=ba$ follows.

\pvn 3.  Hilbert axioms for an ordered field include the following: For $a\neq 0$, and $b$, there exists the unique element $x$ such that
\[ax=b.\]

 Figure \ref{hilbert7} (left) illustrates the construction of the line $\frac ba$.
The same figure shows that $\frac ba a=b$. Since multiplication is commutative, it follows that $\frac ba$ solves the equation $ax=b$.

Setting $b=1$ in this axiom implies the existence of the inverse $a^{-1}$; see Fig. \ref{hilbert7} (right).


