The way of arriving at Thales' theorem in this system is similar to Hilbert and Hartshorne: first introducing points arithmetic, then defining proportions \cite{ref_szt}. The difference is that in the Schwabh\"{a}user-Szmielew-Tarski's system only points are the primitive objects. Before defining arithmetic operations over points, Schwabh\"{a}user-Szmielew-Tarski introduced the concept of axis. For the sake of simplicity, we can say that the axis is points $o$ and $e$ (or non-collinear points $ o $, $ e $, $ e '$), where $ o $ is the beginning of the axis, $ e $ ( and $ e' $ ) unit points.

\begin{definition} \label{ad_bo}
Take in the plane three fixed points $o$, $e$ and $e'$, that define two fixed straight lines $oe$ and $oe'$. Let $e$, $a$ and $b$ be colinear points. Now in order to define the sum of the points $a$ and $b$, we construct $aa'\parallel ee'$ and draw through $a'$ a parallel to $oe$ and through $b$
a parallel to $oe'$. Let these two parallels intersect in $c^*$. Finally, draw through $c^*$ a
straight line parallel to the $ ee'$. Let this parallel cut the fixed line $oe$  in $c$. Then $c$ is called the sum of the $a$ and $b$ (see Fig. \ref{tarski1}).
\end{definition}

