Even when the disk starts out precisely at the symmetry axis $\mathsf{y=0}$ and the gravitational torque vanishes, any misalignment with the symmetry axis produced either by noise or by a tiny residual displacement, causes a gravitational torque. When this torque surpasses the torque caused by static friction it generates angular momentum $\mathcal{J}$ (Figure \ref{slow}a) while pushing the disk sideways, Figure \ref{YZ14}a. Because gravitational torque is proportional to $Y$, the smaller $Y$ is initially, the higher the disk can be displaced upwards before this torque overcomes static friction and the deeper it falls down when eventually overturning, see the differing heights reached by the disk in successive cycles (Figure \ref{slow}b). During this phase, friction lends the disk some metastability. In its following stable phase ($Z<0$) oscillations decay until angular momentum is depleted. At some point, when nearly all angular momentum is lost, static friction arrests the disk's rotary motion. While still nonzero, $Y$ can then become very small, defining the disk's `new' initial $Y$ in the next cycle (Figures \ref{YZ14}a and \ref{slow}b). 

