As argued before, since $Z$ is a subset of $A$, this will imply that $W,X,Y$ are elements of $\mathcal I$. Moreover, $Z$ cannot contain an element of $\mathcal I$ since $A$ does not contain one. Consequently this would mean that $A\notin\mathcal V(\mathcal I,\mathcal J)$ as $Z$ contradicts its minimality. Thus no such $Z$ exists, proving that $\widehat{\mathcal V(\mathcal I, \mathcal J)}\subseteq \mathcal V(\widehat{\mathcal I},\widehat{\mathcal J})$. 

For the other direction, let $A\in \mathcal V(\widehat{\mathcal I},\widehat{\mathcal J})$. Then there exist $P,Q,R\in\widehat{\mathcal I}$ such that $A,P,Q,R$ form a diamond in which $A$ is the top point, as illustrated below.
