\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we extend the research on time-consistent stochastic control problems with higher-order moments, as formulated by [Y. Wang et al. SIAM J. Control. Optim., 63 (2025), in press].
We consider a linear controlled dynamic equation with state-dependent diffusion,
and let the sum of a conventional mean-variance utility and a fairly general function of higher-order central moments be the objective functional.
We obtain both the sufficiency and necessity of the equilibrium condition for an open-loop Nash equilibrium control (ONEC), under some continuity and integrability assumptions that are more relaxed and natural than those employed before.
Notably, we derive an extended version of the stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem for necessity, 
because the equilibrium condition is represented by some diagonal processes generated by a flow of backward stochastic differential equations whose the data do not necessarily satisfy the usual square-integrability.
Based on the derived equilibrium condition, we obtain the algebra equation for a deterministic ONEC.
In particular, we find that the mean-variance equilibrium strategy is an ONEC for our higher-order moment problem if and only if the objective functional satisfies a homogeneity condition.
\end{abstract}

\noindent {\bf Keywords:} stochastic control, higher-order moment, open-loop Nash equilibrium control, sufficiency and necessity, homogeneity condition

\vspace{3mm}

\noindent {\bf AMS2010 classification:} Primary: 93E20, 91G80; Secondary: 91B08, 49N90
\vspace{3mm}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec: Introduction}

Since \cite{Markowitz-1952} pioneered the mean-variance analysis domain in the 1950s,
the mean maximization and variance minimization problem has attracted much attention in field such as mathematical finance and control engineering,
and research on the mean-variance efficiency frontier and efficient portfolios has continued in recent years.
Due to multiobjective convex optimization theory, the process of seeking an efficient portfolio can be reduced to solving a mean-variance utility optimization problem (see \cite[p. 337]{Yong-Zhou-1999}).
Therefore, mean-variance objective functionals are becoming increasingly common in studies related to stochastic control and dynamic optimization problems.

On the one hand, to obtain theoretical developments and implement practical applications, an increasing number of characterizations of risk, such as skewness and kurtosis, should be considered.
This is also a natural extension of mean-variance analysis.
In addition, \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} provided several reasons to incorporate higher-order moments into objective functions, which can be summarized as follows.
\begin{itemize}
\item The distribution of a contingent claim can be characterized by its higher-order moments under some regularity conditions.
\item Through Taylor expansion, the general utility function of a contingent claim can be approximately expressed as an objective function with higher-order moments.
\item Investigating the higher-order moment problem is a heuristic approach for solving portfolio selection problems with fairly general nonlinear law-dependent preferences (see Example 4.6 therein).
\item An objective function with higher-order moments can be treated as a penalty function of the deviation of a contingent claim,
and an appropriate higher-moment problem might provide a potential solution to the original problem with a fairly general penalty function (see Remark 5.10 therein).
\end{itemize}
However, plugging a higher-order moment into the objective functional of an optimization problem, whether static or dynamic, usually leads to many technical difficulties when deriving a solution;
see also \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} and its references.
For example, \cite{Boissaux-Schiltz-2010} derived the stochastic maximum principle for a mean-variance-skewness-kurtosis problem but failed to derive an explicit solution.
In fact, providing and verifying an Ansatz for the value function or value process of a higher-order moment problem is not easy
because the ``first-order derivative'' optimality condition is usually a nonlinear equation, and so its solution cannot be explicitly expressed.
When the target problem cannot be reduced to a convex problem, the abovementioned nonlinear equation may produce corner solutions, which increases the complexity of listing cases.

On the other hand, the presence of variance, as well as other higher-order moments, in the objective functional usually implies that the optimal solutions exhibit time-inconsistency.
The study of time-inconsistency can also be traced back to the 1950s; see \cite{Strotz-1955} for the consumption optimization problem with non-exponential intertemporal utility discounting.
The major negative effect of time-inconsistency is that the decision maker immediately changes their plan to realize a temporary maximum/minimum once the information is refreshed and finally implements a myopia strategy without any optimality.
For example, one can follow the line of \cite{Zhou-Li-2000} to solve a mean-variance optimization problem with a new initial epoch and then find the difference between the new optimal strategy and the primal strategy.
The usual approach for addressing time-inconsistency is to take the game-theoretic perspective and formulate the problem as a game between the incarnations of the decision maker at different time instants.
See also \cite[Section 2.3]{Bjork-Murgoci-Zhou-2014}.
An equilibrium point is treated as a time-consistent solution because any spike deviation from it will not be ``significantly'' better.
If a control process (resp. a feedback scheme) is sought, an equilibrium point is named an open-loop (resp. closed-loop) Nash equilibrium control.
The related works include \cite{Yong-2011,Yong-2012,Bjork-Murgoci-2014,Bjork-Murgoci-Zhou-2014,Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017,Wei-2017,Wang-Zheng-2021} for closed-loop Nash equilibrium control (CNEC),
\cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017,Sun-Guo-2019,Alia-2019,Wang-2019,Alia-2020} for open-loop Nash equilibrium control (ONEC) and \cite{Yong-2017,Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} for both types.

As a well-known result, for a classic mean-variance portfolio problem with deterministic parameters, the ONEC and CNEC are the same deterministic function; see also \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017}.
Our previous work, \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, extended this result to a class of higher-order moment problems.
That is, a deterministic function exists as the time-consistent solution for a higher-order moment problem under certain conditions, although it is difficult to find its optimal solution. 
However, those conditions are so strong that they seem unnatural.
For example, the $\mathbb{L}^{ 2n-2 + \epsilon }$-integrability of the terminal state value was proposed, but the objective functional includes the moments only up to the $n$-th order.
This requirement serves the square-integrability in the backward stochastic differential equations (BSDEs), as in most of the literature.
Apart from that, the global Lipschitz continuity prohibits the ratio between higher-order moments, including standardized moments such as skewness and kurtosis, from being included in the objective function.
This severely limits its potential application scope.
On the other hand, for open-loop equilibria, we only showed the sufficiency of a complicated equilibrium condition in \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, 
which contained the limit of the ratio of the conditional expectation of an integral to the variational factor.
In view of \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017,Wang-2019}, the equilibrium condition may be supposed to be expressed as some equations and inequalities of the diagonal processes generated by a flow of BSDEs indexed by different initial epochs.

Observing the abovementioned drawbacks in our previous work, we make the corresponding improvements in this paper.
We suppose that the objective functional is the sum of the classic mean-variance utility and a general deterministic function of higher-order central moments,
and we aim to characterize and then seek an ONEC.
Instead of the global Lipschitz continuity condition, we impose a local Lipschitz continuity condition on this general function to allow the higher-order moments of even orders (e.g., the variance) in the denominator.
As the objective functional includes the moments up to the $2n$-th order, we merely require the ``$\mathbb{L}^{2n}$-integrability'' of the terminal state value,
or equivalently, $\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{0}^{T} | {u}_{t} |^{2} dt )^{n} ] < \infty $ for all admissible controls $u$.
Notably, for brevity of notation, we choose $2n$ as the highest order, and no additional essential question will be encountered if we replace the even number $2n$ with a general integer $n \ge 2$.
As a consequence, the terminal value, i.e., the data, in each adjoint BSDE is merely $\mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta }$-integrable.
By exploiting the theory of $\mathbb{L}^{p}$ solutions of BSDEs (see \cite{Briand-Delyon-Hu-Pardoux-Stoica-2003,ElKaroui-Peng-Quenez-1997,Chen-2010}),
we show in \Cref{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process} that the diagonal process is also ``$\mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta }$-integrable''.
Although \cite[Lemma 3.4]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017}, as a stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem, is not applicable to our problem, we mirror its proof and obtain its analog version for the weaker ``$\mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta }$-integrability'' condition;
see \Cref{lem: stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem}.
Then, we show both the sufficiency and necessity of the equilibrium condition in \Cref{thm: maximum principle}, which is explicitly and briefly expressed by the exact value of the diagonal processes, for open-loop equilibria.
On the other hand, since \cite[Example 5.7 and Theorem 5.9]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} implies that 
the mean-variance equilibrium strategy is also an ONEC for the mean-variance-standardized moments objective functionals,
interested readers may wonder under which conditions this phenomenon could be repeated for other higher-order moment objective functionals.
In this paper, we provide the sufficient and necessary condition (see \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} and \Cref{thm: MV solution is ONEC}), which is based on the derived equivalent condition for open-loop equilibria.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows.
In \Cref{sec: Problem formulation}, we formulate our control problem.
In \Cref{sec: Mathematical preliminaries}, we provide some mathematical preliminaries for characterizing the open-loop equilibria,
including the perturbation argument, the diagonal processes generated by a flow of BSDEs and an extended version of the stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem.
In \Cref{sec: Sufficient and necessary condition for non-trivial ONEC}, we show the sufficiency and necessity of the equilibrium condition for open-loop equilibria.
In \Cref{sec: Closed-form solution}, we consider some particular cases and reduce the equilibrium condition to some integral equations and inequalities,
and then show that the mean-variance equilibrium strategy is an ONEC for our higher-order moment problem under a so-called homogeneity condition.
To end this paper, we present concluding remarks in \Cref{sec: Concluding remark}.

\section{Problem formulation}
\label{sec: Problem formulation}

Let $T$ be a fixed finite time horizon, $( \Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P} )$ be a complete probability space, and $\mathbb{E}$ be the expectation operator.
Suppose that $W := \{ {W}_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] }$ is a one-dimensional standard Brownian motion defined on $( \Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P} )$
and generates the right-continuous complete natural filtration $\mathbb{F} := \{ \mathcal{F}_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] }$, and $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ \cdot ] := \mathbb{E} [ \cdot | \mathcal{F}_{t} ]$ is the conditional expectation operator.
For $p > 1$, we introduce the following notation of spaces: 
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathbb{L}^{p}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega )$ denotes the set of all $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-measurable random variables $f: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\mathbb{E} [ | f |^{p} ] < \infty $;
\item $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable processes $f: [ 0,T ] \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$ with
      $\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{0}^{T} | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{2} ds )^{p/2} ] < \infty $;
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. sample continuous processes $f \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$
      with $\mathbb{E} [ \sup_{ s \in [ 0,T ] } | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{p} ] < \infty $;
\item $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable processes $f: [ 0,T ) \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$
      with $\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{0}^{\tau } | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{2} ds )^{p/2} ] < \infty $ for any fixed $\tau \in ( 0,T )$;
\item $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable processes $f: [ 0,T ) \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$
      with $\esssup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } \mathbb{E} [ | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{p} ] < \infty $ for any fixed $\tau \in ( 0,T )$;
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}, loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. continuous processes $f \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$;
      with $\mathbb{E} [ \sup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{p} ] < \infty $ for any fixed $\tau \in ( 0,T )$;
\item $\mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T ) \cap \mathbb{L}^{\infty }_{loc} ( 0,T )$ denotes the set of all deterministic measurable function $f: [ 0,T ] \to \mathbb{R}$ 
      such that $\int_{0}^{T} | {f}_{t} |^{2} dt < \infty $ and $\esssup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } | {f}_{s} | < \infty $ for any fixed $\tau \in ( 0,T )$;
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable random fields $f: [ 0,T ) \times \Omega \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$
      such that $f ( \cdot, \cdot, x ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $f ( t,\omega, \cdot )$ is twice continuously differentiable for all $( t, \omega ) \in [ 0,T ] \times \Omega $.;
\item $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable random fields $f: [ 0,T ) \times \Omega \times \Omega \to \mathbb{R}$
      such that $f ( \cdot, \cdot, x ) \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $f ( t,\omega, \cdot )$ is twice continuously differentiable for all $( t, \omega ) \in [ 0,T ] \times \Omega $.
\end{itemize}
In addition, we let ${\alpha }_{2j-1} (y) := 0$ and ${\alpha }_{2j} (y) := (2j-1)!! {y}^{j}$ for all positive integers $j$,
where $(2j-1)!!$ is the double factorial of $2j-1$, i.e., $(2j-1)!! = (2j-1) \times (2j-3) \times \cdots \times 3 \times 1$,
and we write $\vec{\alpha } (y) := \big( {\alpha }_{2} (y), {\alpha }_{3} (y), \ldots, {\alpha }_{2n} (y) \big)$ for the sake of brevity.
Notably, to mitigate misunderstandings, we highly suggest that readers keep in mind that the argument ${z}_{j}$ in the sequel corresponds to ${\alpha }_{j} (y)$ and the $j$-th central moment unless otherwise mentioned.

We consider the following stochastic differential equation (SDE) for the state-control pair $( X,u )$:
\begin{equation}
d {X}_{t} = ( {A}_{t} {X}_{t} + {B}_{t} {u}_{t} + {C}_{t} ) dt + ( {I}_{t} {X}_{t} + {D}_{t} {u}_{t} + {F}_{t} ) d {W}_{t}, \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ], \quad
  {X}_{0} = {x}_{0},
\label{eq: linear controlled SDE :eq}
\end{equation}
where $( A, B, C, I, D, F )$ are deterministic bounded functions with $\int_{\tau }^{T} | {B}_{s} | ds > 0$ for every $\tau \in [ 0,T )$ and $| {D}_{t} | \ge \iota $ for every $t \in [ 0,T ]$ and some $\iota > 0$.
Let ${X}^{u}$ denote the unique $\mathbb{F}$-adapted strong solution of \cref{eq: linear controlled SDE :eq}.
As $u \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$ leads to ${X}^{u}_{T} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{p} ( \Omega )$ for $p \ge 2$ due to Burkholder's inequality,
we consider the following objective functional $J: \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) ) \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\gamma \in \mathbb{R}_{+}$ and some (sufficiently large) positive integer $n$:
\begin{align*}
J (u) & := \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \\
      &~\quad - \varphi \big( \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big], \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{3} \big], 
                              \ldots, \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2n} \big] \big).
\end{align*}
This is a sum of the classic mean-variance utility ${J}_{MV} (u) = \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \gamma \Var [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] / 2$
and a continuously differential function $\varphi ( {z}_{2}, {z}_{3}, \ldots, {z}_{2n} )$ for the higher-order central moments $\{ \mathbb{E} [ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j} ] \}_{ j = 2,3,\ldots,2n }$.
Furthermore, we introduce the following functionals on $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ indexed by $t$:
\begin{align}
{J}^{t} (u) & := \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \notag \\
            &~\quad - \varphi \big( \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big], \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{3} \big], 
                                    \ldots, \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2n} \big] \big).
\label{eq: objective functional :eq}
\end{align}
That is, replacing all expectation operators $\mathbb{E}$ with $\mathbb{E}_{t}$ in the expression of $J(u)$ produces ${J}^{t} (u)$.
As ${J}^{t}$ is a straightforward and natural continuous embedding, it is supposed to evaluate all $u \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ for the initial epoch $t \in [ 0,T )$.
Then, time-inconsistency emerges, as mentioned in \Cref{sec: Introduction}.
To address this time-inconsistency, we refer to \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} and investigate the ONECs for ${J}_{MVSM}$, the definition of which is stated below.

\begin{definition}\label{def: open-loop Nash equilibrium control}
$\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ is an ONEC, if
\begin{equation}
0 \le \liminf_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0}
      \frac{ {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) }{\varepsilon }, \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ),\quad \forall \zeta \in \mathbb{L}^{2n}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega ),
\label{eq: open-loop Nash equilibrium control :eq}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }$ is a spike variation of $\bar{u}$ given by $\bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{s} = \bar{u}_{s} + \zeta {1}_{\{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ) \}}$.
\end{definition}

Furthermore, we say that $u \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ is non-trivial unless ${I}_{t} {X}^{u}_{t} + {D}_{t} {u}_{t} + {F}_{t} = 0$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. for a $t \in [ \tau, T ]$ with some $\tau \in [ 0,T )$.
Thus, $u$ is trivial if and only if ${X}^{u}_{T} = \mathbb{E}_{\tau } [ {X}^{u}_{T} ]$ $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. for some $\tau \in [ 0,T )$.
Notably, a trivial ONEC is not necessarily characterized by \Cref{thm: maximum principle}.
For example, for $\varphi = | {z}_{1} |^{1/2}$, namely, for the following mean-variance-standard deviation objective functional,
\begin{equation*}
{J}_{MVSD}^{t} (u) = \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] 
                   - \big( \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \big)^{ \frac{1}{2} },
\end{equation*}
there is a trivial ONEC $\bar{u}$ satisfying ${I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} = 0$ for all $t \in [ 0,T ]$. This is due to
\begin{align*}
{J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
& = \zeta \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } {B}_{s} ds
  - \frac{\gamma }{2} {\zeta }^{2} \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \\
& \quad - | \zeta | \bigg( \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{2} } \\
& \le - | \zeta | \iota {e}^{ - T ( \sup |A| + \frac{1}{\iota } \sup |B| \sup |I| ) } {\varepsilon }^{ \frac{1}{2} } + O ( \varepsilon ),
\end{align*}
which implies that $( {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) ) / \varepsilon \to + \infty $ as $\varepsilon \downarrow 0$ for any $\zeta \ne 0$.
However, in this case, ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} = 1 - 0 \times \infty $ and ${Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} = - \infty $ for the BSDEs \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} do not satisfy the related assumptions.
In general, if $| \varphi ( \vec{\alpha } (0) ) | < \infty $ is well-defined and $\bar{u}$ is trivial, then through straightforward calculations, one obtains that
\begin{align}
{J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
& = \zeta \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } {B}_{s} ds
  - \frac{\gamma }{2} {\zeta }^{2} \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \notag \\
& \quad + \varphi \big( \vec{\alpha } (0) \big) - \varphi \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( {\zeta }^{2} \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg)
\label{eq: perturbation argument for trivial ONEC :eq}
\end{align}
for any $t \in [ 0,T )$ such that ${I}_{s} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} + {D}_{s} \bar{u}_{s} + {F}_{s} = 0$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., and $s \in [ t,T ]$.
As the fairly general $\varphi $ might be ill-posed around the origin, one cannot obtain a universal asymptotic estimate for ${J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )$ akin to \Cref{lem: spike perturbation}.
Therefore, we consider only non-trivial ONECs in the main body of this paper.
Readers interested in trivial ONECs can refer to \Cref{rem: trivial ONEC} for the potential methodology.

Interested readers can also refer to \cite{Bjork-Murgoci-Zhou-2014,Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017} and seek closed-loop equilibria.
In fact, \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} studied sufficient conditions for both ONECs and CNECs within a fairly general framework.
However, the mean-variance-standardized moment objective function
\begin{equation*}
{J}_{MVSM} (u) = \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big]
               - \sum_{j=3}^{n} {\gamma }_{j} \frac{ \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j} \big] }
                                                   { \big( \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \big)^{ \frac{j}{2} } }
\end{equation*}
does not satisfy the global Lipschitz continuity and boundedness condition employed by \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}.
As a consequence, an interesting implication of \cite[Example 5.7 and Theorem 5.9]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, which shows that the ONEC for ${J}_{MVSM}$ is the same as that for ${J}_{MV}$, seems less rigorous.
Nevertheless, the abovementioned implication can be justified rigorously
because the maximum principle for an ONEC (as well as the extended Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equations for a CNEC) relies only on the propositions applied at the equilibrium point.
We make a technical modification and adopt the following \Cref{ass: local Lipschitz continuity} throughout this paper (we do not mention it again in the statements of the lemmas and theorems); this assumption can also be recognized as the local Lipschitz continuity of $\varphi $.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass: local Lipschitz continuity}
$\varphi $ is Lipschitz continuous on every compact subset of $\mathbb{R}_{+} \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}_{+} \times \mathbb{R} \times \cdots \times \mathbb{R}_{+}$.
\end{assumption} 

Heuristically, when \Cref{ass: local Lipschitz continuity} holds, $I \equiv 0$ and $( A,B,C,D,F )$ are continuous,
one can still mirror the steps described in \cite[Sections 3 and 4]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} to arrive at the sufficient condition for ONECs and CNECs
and the consequential results presented in \cite[Sections 5.1 and 5.2]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}.
That is, a particular path- and state-independent function can be the candidate for both an ONEC and a CNEC; and ${J}_{MVSM}$ and ${J}_{MV}$ may have the same ONEC and CNEC, as follows:
\begin{equation}
\bar{u}_{t} = \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ \gamma | {D}_{t} |^{2} } {e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } - \frac{ {F}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} }.
\label{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq}
\end{equation}
In addition to \Cref{ass: local Lipschitz continuity}, the following \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} is of major concern, under which ${J}$ and ${J}_{MV}$ do have the same ONEC in the case with $I \equiv 0$.

\begin{assumption}[Homogeneity condition]\label{ass: homogeneity condition}
$\frac{d}{dy} \varphi ( \vec{\alpha } (y) ) = 0$ for any $y \in \mathbb{R}_{+}$.
\end{assumption}

Notably, ${J}_{MVSM}$ satisfies \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition}, since its corresponding $\varphi $ is a linear combination of ${z}_{j} / {z}_{2}^{j/2}$
such that ${\alpha }_{j} (y) / ( {\alpha }_{2} (y) )^{j/2}$ is independent of $y$.
In the same manner, one can find infinitely many objective functionals that satisfy \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition}
by letting $\varphi $ be a linear combination of ${z}_{ 2 {j}_{1} }^{ {k}_{1} } {z}_{ 2 {j}_{2} }^{ {k}_{2} } \ldots {z}_{ 2 {j}_{m} }^{ {k}_{m} }$
with ${j}_{i} \in \mathbb{N}_{+}$, ${k}_{i} \in \mathbb{R}$ and $\sum_{i=1}^{m} {j}_{i} {k}_{i} = 0$, which leads to
\begin{equation*}
\big( {\alpha }_{ 2 {j}_{1} } (y) \big)^{ {k}_{1} } \big( {\alpha }_{ 2 {j}_{2} } (y) \big)^{ {k}_{2} } \ldots \big( {\alpha }_{ 2 {j}_{m} } (y) \big)^{ {k}_{m} }
\equiv \big( ( 2 {j}_{1} - 1 ) !! \big)^{ {k}_{1} } \big( ( 2 {j}_{2} - 1 ) !! \big)^{ {k}_{2} } \ldots \big( ( 2 {j}_{m} - 1 ) !! \big)^{ {k}_{m} }
\end{equation*}
for any $y$.
This is also the reason that we name \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} the homogeneity condition.
For example, $\varphi $ could be the monomial ${z}_{8} {z}_{4}^{-1} {z}_{2}^{-2}$, and then the corresponding objective functional
\begin{align*}
J (u) & = \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big]
        - \frac{ \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{8} \big] }
               { \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{4} \big] \big( \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \big)^{2} } \\
      & = \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big]
        - \frac{ \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{8} \big] }{ \big( \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \big)^{4} }
          \bigg/ \frac{ \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{4} \big] }{ \big( \mathbb{E} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] \big)^{2} }; 
\end{align*}
that is, $J$ is the mean-variance utility minus the ratio of the eighth standardized moment to the kurtosis.

\section{Mathematical preliminaries}
\label{sec: Mathematical preliminaries}

\subsection{Perturbation argument}

Due to the linear controlled SDE \cref{eq: linear controlled SDE :eq},
we introduce the following (first-order) variational equation:
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
d {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{s} & = ( {A}_{s} {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{s} + {1}_{\{ s \in ( t, t + \varepsilon ) \}} {B}_{s} \zeta ) ds 
                                      + ( {I}_{s} {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{s} + {1}_{\{ s \in ( t, t + \varepsilon ) \}} {D}_{s} \zeta ) d {W}_{s}, \quad \forall s \in [ t,T ]; \\
  {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{s} & = 0, \quad \forall s \in [ 0,t ],
\end{aligned} \right.
\label{eq: variational equation :eq}
\end{equation}
which results in a slightly simpler technique than that in \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} and \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, where the second-order variational equations is also considered.
For any given $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( [ 0,T ]; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega ) )$,
${X}^{ \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } } - {X}^{ \bar{u} } = {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }$ follows from \cref{eq: linear controlled SDE :eq}, and
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} ] = O ( \varepsilon ), \quad
\mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} ] )^{2j} \big] = O ( {\varepsilon }^{j} ), \quad \forall j \in \mathbb{N}_{+}.
\end{equation*}
For the sake of brevity, hereafter we write ${\varphi }_{j} ( {z}_{2}, \ldots, {z}_{2n} ) := \frac{ \partial \varphi }{ \partial {z}_{j} } ( {z}_{2}, \ldots, {z}_{2n} )$ and
\begin{equation*}
{f}^{t,u} = f \Big( \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big], \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{3} \big], \ldots, 
                    \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2n} \big] \Big), \quad \forall f = \varphi, {\varphi }_{j}.
\end{equation*}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: spike perturbation}
For any given non-trivial $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$, $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\zeta \in \mathbb{L}^{2n}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega )$ and a sufficiently small $\varepsilon > 0$,
\begin{align*}
  {J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
& = \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} ] 
  - \gamma \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] ) {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} \big]
  - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} )^{2} \big] \\
& \quad - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \Big( ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} - \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} \big] \Big) 
                                                 {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} \Big] {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} } \\
& \quad - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} \binom{j}{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-2} ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} )^{2} \Big] {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} } 
  + o ( \varepsilon ).
\end{align*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Through a straightforward calculation, one can obtain
\begin{align*}
& \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } }_{T} ] )^{j} \big]
  - \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j} \big] \\
& = j \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} ( {y}^{\varepsilon }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} ] ) \big] \\
& \quad + \binom{j}{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-2} ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} ] )^{2} \big] 
        + o ( \varepsilon ) \\
& = j \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \big( ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} - \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} \big] \big) {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} \Big] \\
& \quad + \binom{j}{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-2} ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} )^{2} \big] 
        + o ( \varepsilon )
\end{align*}
for any integer $j \ge 2$.
As \Cref{ass: local Lipschitz continuity} holds and $\bar{u}$ is non-trivial,
one can apply Taylor expansion to \cref{eq: objective functional :eq} in conjunction with the above moment estimates to arrive at the desired result.
\end{proof}


\subsection{BSDEs and diagonal processes}
\label{sec: BSDEs and diagonal processes}

Let us introduce a flow of linear BSDEs indexed by $t \in [ 0,T )$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
d {Y}^{t,u}_{s} & = - ( {A}_{s} {Y}^{t,u}_{s} + {I}_{s} \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{s} ) ds + \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{s} d {W}_{s}, ~ \forall s \in [ 0,T ], \\
  {Y}^{t,u}_{T} & = 1 - \gamma ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )
                      - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j-1} - \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j-1} \big] \Big) {\varphi }_{j}^{t,u}; \\
d {Z}^{t,u}_{s} & = - ( 2 {A}_{s} {Z}^{t,u}_{s} + | {I}_{s} |^{2} {Z}^{t,u}_{s} + 2 {I}_{s} \mathcal{Z}^{t,u}_{s} ) ds + \mathcal{Z}^{t,u}_{s} d {W}_{s}, ~ \forall s \in [ 0,T ], \\
  {Z}^{t,u}_{T} & = - \frac{\gamma }{2}
                    - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} \binom{j}{2} ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j-2} {\varphi }_{j}^{t,u}.
\end{aligned} \right. 
\label{eq: BSDEs :eq}
\end{equation}
Considering the well-posedness of \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq}, we employ the following assumption.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity}
For the given non-trivial $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$,
there exists a sufficiently small $\delta \in ( 0, \frac{1}{2n-1} )$ such that
${\varphi }_{j}^{ \cdot, \bar{u} } \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ \frac{ 2 n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2 n - ( j - 1 ) ( 1 + \delta ) } } ( \Omega ) )$ for all $j$.
\end{assumption}

Notably, one can impose a stronger assumption, e.g., that ${\varphi }_{j}^{ \cdot, \bar{u} } \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 2n ( 1 + \rho ) } ( \Omega ) )$ uniformly for all $j$ and some $\rho > 0$.
For any $u \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ satisfying \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity},
we have that ${Y}^{t,u}_{T}, {Z}^{t,u}_{T} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega )$.
Consequently, one can find the unique solution $( {Y}^{t,u}, \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}, {Z}^{t,u}, \mathcal{Z}^{t,u} )$ of \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} in
\begin{equation*}
{C}_{\mathbb{F}} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times 
{C}_{\mathbb{F}} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big)
\end{equation*}
according to \cite{Briand-Delyon-Hu-Pardoux-Stoica-2003} for the $\mathbb{L}^{p}$ solutions of general BSDEs.
Related applications and theoretical improvements can be found in \cite{ElKaroui-Peng-Quenez-1997,Chen-2010}, etc.

Owing to the linearity of \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq},
we are able to provide the analytical expression of $( {Y}^{t,u}, \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}, {Z}^{t,u}, \mathcal{Z}^{t,u} )$ by change of measure.
Let us introduce the equivalent martingale measures $\mathbb{P}^{(i)}$ with $i = 1,2$,
under which $\{ {W}^{(i)}_{t} := {W}_{t} - i \int_{0}^{t} {I}_{s} ds \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] }$ is a one-dimensional standard Brownian motion:
\begin{equation*}
\frac{ d \mathbb{P}^{(i)} }{ d \mathbb{P} } \Big|_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } = {e}^{ i \int_{0}^{T} {I}_{v} d {W}_{v} - \frac{1}{2} {i}^{2} \int_{0}^{T} | {I}_{v} |^{2} dv }.
\end{equation*}
Let $\mathbb{E}^{(i)}$ be the expectation operator for $\mathbb{P}^{(i)}$, and $\mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ \cdot ] := \mathbb{E}^{(i)} [ \cdot | \mathcal{F}_{t} ]$.
Then,
\begin{equation}
{Y}^{t,u}_{s} = {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{s} [ {Y}^{t,u}_{T} ], \quad 
{Z}^{t,u}_{s} = {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} ( 2 {A}_{v} + | {I}_{v} |^{2} ) dv } \mathbb{E}^{(2)}_{s} [ {Z}^{t,u}_{T} ], 
\quad \forall s \in [ 0,T ], ~ \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ \forall t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: BSDEs solution :eq}
\end{equation}
As the unique solution for the BSDEs $\mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( {X}^{u}_{T} )^{k} ] = ( {X}^{u}_{T} )^{k} - \int_{t}^{T} {\xi }^{i,k,u} ( d {W}_{s} - i {I}_{s} ds )$ (indexed by $i = 1,2$ and $k = 1,2,\ldots,2n-1$) 
with the data $( {X}^{u}_{T} )^{k} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{2n/k} ( \Omega )$,
the pair $( \{ \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( {X}^{u}_{T} )^{k} ] \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] }, {\xi }^{i,k,u} ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/k} ( \Omega ) ) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/k} ( \Omega ) )$.
This is also recognized as the $\mathbb{L}^{p}$-martingale representation under $\mathbb{P}^{(i)}$ (see \cite{Lin-1995}).
As a result, since \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} gives
\begin{align*}
d \Big( {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } {Y}^{t,u}_{s} \Big) & = {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{s} d {W}^{(1)}_{s}, \\
d \Big( {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} ( 2 {A}_{v} + | {I}_{v} |^{2} ) dv } {Z}^{t,u}_{s} \Big) & = {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} ( 2 {A}_{v} + | {I}_{v} |^{2} ) dv } \mathcal{Z}^{t,u}_{s} d {W}^{(2)}_{s},
\end{align*}
in conjunction with \cref{eq: BSDEs solution :eq}, we have that
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
& \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{s} = - {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \bigg( \gamma {\xi }^{1,1,u}_{s} + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} \sum_{k=1}^{j-1} j \binom{j-1}{k} {\xi }^{1,k,u}_{s} ( \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j-1-k} {\varphi }_{j}^{t,u} \bigg), \\
& \mathcal{Z}^{t,u}_{s} = - {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \sum_{j=3}^{2n} \sum_{k=1}^{j-2} \binom{j}{2} \binom{j-2}{k} {\xi }^{2,k,u}_{s} ( \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{j-2-k} {\varphi }_{j}^{t,u}, \\
& a.e. ~ s \in [ 0,T ], ~ \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ \forall t \in [ 0,T ).
\end{aligned} \right.
\label{eq: BSDEs solution cal :eq}
\end{equation}

However, the uniqueness and integrability of the diagonal process $\{ \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) }$ should be carefully justified, which is used to prove the necessity of \Cref{thm: maximum principle,thm: ONEC}.
In general, one might attempt to arbitrarily choose $\mathcal{Y}^{t}_{t}$ for every $t$, because we only have the uniqueness of $\mathcal{Y}^{t,u}$, which merely means that
$\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{0}^{T} | \mathcal{Y}^{t,u}_{s} - \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^{t,u}_{s} |^{2} ds )^{ \frac{ 1 + \delta }{2} } ] = 0$
for another solution $( \hat{Y}^{t,u}, \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^{t,u}, \hat{Z}^{t,u}, \hat{\mathcal{Z}}^{t,u} )$ of \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq}.
Fortunately, we have the following \Cref{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process} to guarantee the uniqueness and integrability of the diagonal processes in a certain sense.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process}
Suppose that \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} holds for a given $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$.
Then, the flow of the BSDEs given by \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} generates a diagonal process quadruplet $\{ ( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \mathcal{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} ) \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) }$ in
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times 
\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} \big( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big).
\end{equation*}
Moreover, if $( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }, {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \mathcal{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} } )$
and $( \hat{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \hat{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \hat{\mathcal{Z}}^{ t, \bar{u} } )$ are two solutions of \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq}, then
\begin{equation*}
( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \mathcal{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} ) 
= ( \hat{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \hat{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \hat{\mathcal{Z}}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} ),
\quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ).
\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
It follows from \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} and \cref{eq: BSDEs solution :eq} that
\begin{align}
{e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} 
= 1 + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ]
    + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} \big] {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} }
    - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (-1)^{j-1} ( \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1} {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} } \notag \\
    - \gamma \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{s} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] 
    - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} \sum_{k=1}^{j-1} j \binom{j-1}{k} (-1)^{j-1-k} ( \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-1-k} {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} } \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{s} [ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} )^{k} ], \notag \\
\forall s \in [ 0,T ], \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s. \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: BSDEs solution expansion :eq}
\end{align}
For ease of notation and to mitigate potential misunderstandings, we rewrite \cref{eq: BSDEs solution expansion :eq} as
\begin{equation}
{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} ( \omega ) = {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} ( \omega ) {M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} ( \omega ),
\quad \forall s \in [ 0,T ] \times ( \Omega \setminus \mathcal{N}_{t} ), \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: polynomial expression of Y :eq}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{N}_{t} \subset \Omega $ is some $t$-dependent $\mathbb{P}$-null subset.
Each $( {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }, {M}^{ k, \bar{u} } )$ is not necessarily $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. continuous but has a $\mathbb{P}$-indistinguishable version
$( \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }, \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} } ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ \frac{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) } } ( \Omega ) ) \times {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/k} ( \Omega ) )$
with $\bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t}$ being a linear combination of $( \bar{M}^{ 1, \bar{u} }_{t} )^{j-1-k} {\varphi }^{ t, \bar{u} }_{j}$.
Notably, the integrability of $\bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }$ follows from \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity}.
For any fixed $\tau \in [ 0,T )$,
\begin{align*}
& \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } \sup_{ s \in [ 0,T ] } \bigg| \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} ( {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} {M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} 
                                                                                  - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} ) \bigg|^{ 1 + \delta } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } \\
& \le \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{ 1 + \delta }
                                                 \sup_{ s \in [ 0,T ] } | {M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} - \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ 1 + \delta } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } \\
& \quad + \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{ 1 + \delta }
                                                     \sup_{ s \in [ 0,T ] } | \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ 1 + \delta } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } = 0.
\end{align*}
We introduce the reference form
\begin{equation}
\bar{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} ( \omega ) = {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} ( \omega ) \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} ( \omega ),
\quad \forall s \in [ t,T ] \times \Omega, \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ),
\label{eq: polynomial expression of Y canonical :eq}
\end{equation}
so that $\{ \bar{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) } \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) )$.
By incorporating \cref{eq: polynomial expression of Y :eq} and \cref{eq: polynomial expression of Y canonical :eq}, one obtains
\begin{equation*}
\esssup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{ 1 + \delta } ]
\le {e}^{ ( 1 + \delta ) T \sup |A| } \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } \bigg| \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} ( {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} {M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} \bar{M}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} ) \bigg|^{ 1 + \delta } \bigg]
 = 0
\end{equation*}
for any $\tau \in [ 0,T )$.
Hence, $\{ {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) } \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) )$, and ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} = \bar{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$.

Next, we investigate the integrability and uniqueness of $\{ \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) }$.
Owing to \cref{eq: BSDEs solution cal :eq}, \cref{eq: BSDEs solution expansion :eq} and \cref{eq: polynomial expression of Y :eq},
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} = {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s}, \quad a.e. ~ s \in [ 0,T ], ~ \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ \forall t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: polynomial expression of calY :eq}
\end{equation}
Similar to \cref{eq: polynomial expression of Y canonical :eq}, for every $t \in [ 0,T )$, we introduce 
$\bar{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} = \exp ( \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv ) \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s}$ on $[ 0,T ] \times \Omega $ as the reference form
with $\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{0}^{T} | {\xi}^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} - \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{2} ds )^{n/k} ] = 0$.
Then, for any fixed $\tau \in [ 0,T )$,
\begin{align*}
& \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{0}^{\tau } | \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{\mathcal{Y}}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} dt \Big)^{ \frac{ 1 + \delta }{2} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } \\
& \le {e}^{ T \sup |A| } 
      \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{0}^{\tau } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} | {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} dt \Big)^{ \frac{ 1 + \delta }{2} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } \\
& \quad + {e}^{ T \sup |A| } 
          \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{0}^{\tau } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} | \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} dt \Big)^{ \frac{ 1 + \delta }{2} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{ 1 + \delta } } \\
& \le {e}^{ T \sup |A| } 
      \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0,\tau ] } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{ \frac{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) } } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) } }
                        \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{0}^{T} | {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} dt \Big)^{ \frac{n}{k} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{k}{2n} } \\
& \quad + {e}^{ T \sup |A| } 
          \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ t \in [ 0,\tau ] } | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{ \frac{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) } } \bigg] 
                                   \bigg)^{ \frac{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) } }
      \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{0}^{T} | \bar{\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2} dt \Big)^{ \frac{n}{k} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{k}{2n} } \\
& = 0,
\end{align*}
where the first inequality is due to Minkowski's inequality and the second inequality is due to H\"older's inequality.
Therefore, $\mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} = \bar{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$.
By Minkowski's inequality and H\"older's inequality, we can show that $\{ \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ) } \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) )$.

The previous argument is also valid for $( \hat{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} } )$; therefore, 
one can conclude that $( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} ) = ( \bar{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t}, \bar{\mathcal{Y}}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$.
In the same manner, we can show the desired results for $( {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }, \mathcal{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} } )$
by using ${\varphi }^{ \cdot, \bar{u} }_{j} \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ \frac{ 2 n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2 n - ( j - 2 ) ( 1 + \delta ) } } ( \Omega ) )$ due to \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity}.
Therefore, the proof is complete.
\end{proof}

In addition to the integrability condition of ${\xi}^{1,k,u}$, we impose the following local uniform integrability assumption, which is convenient to verify for the derived ONEC.

\begin{assumption}\label{ass: uniform integrability condition}
For the given $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$,
${\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} } |_{ [ 0,T ) \times \Omega } \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/k} ( \Omega ) )$ for all $k$.
\end{assumption}

\subsection{Stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem}

From the previous subsection, one can see that the square-integrability does not necessarily hold in this paper.
Therefore, we need to extend the stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem for $\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega ) )$ given by \cite[Lemma 3.4]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} to serve our purpose.
As $\mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{p} ( \Omega )$ is separable for all finite $p \ge 1$,
our proof of the following \Cref{lem: stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem} is merely a minor modification of that presented in \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem}
Suppose that $p > 1$ and $Y \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega ) )$.
If $\lim_{ \varepsilon \downarrow 0 } \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds = 0$, a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.,
then ${Y}_{t} = 0$, a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Applying the classic Lebesgue differentiation theorem produces
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{s} |^{p} ] ds = \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{t} |^{p} ], \quad
\lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} \eta ] ds = \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{t} \eta ], \quad a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ),
\end{equation*}
where $\eta $ is arbitrarily chosen in a countable dense subset $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{p} ( \Omega ) \cap \mathbb{L}_{\mathcal{F}_{T}}^{\infty } ( \Omega )$.
Write ${\eta }_{s} = \mathbb{E}_{s} [ \eta ]$, which leads to $\mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} \eta ] = \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} {\eta }_{s} ]$.
By Holder's inequality and Doob's maximal inequality, one obtains
\begin{align*}
      \bigg| \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon }
             \int_{t}^{t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} ( {\eta }_{s} - {\eta }_{t} ) ] ds \bigg|
& \le \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon }
      \bigg( \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{s} |^{p} ] ds \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{p} }
      \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{t + \varepsilon } | {\eta }_{s} - {\eta }_{t} |^{ \frac{p}{p-1} } ds \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{p-1}{p} } \\
& \le \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \bigg( \sup_{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ] } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{s} |^{p} ] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{p} }
      \bigg( \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ] } | {\eta }_{s} - {\eta }_{t} |^{ \frac{p}{p-1} } \bigg] \bigg)^{ \frac{p-1}{p} } \\
& \le p \bigg( \sup_{ s \in [ t, \frac{1}{2} (t+T) ] } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{s} |^{p} ] \bigg)^{ \frac{1}{p} }
      \bigg( \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \mathbb{E} \big[ | {\eta }_{ t + \varepsilon } - {\eta }_{t} |^{ \frac{p}{p-1} } \big] \bigg)^{ \frac{p-1}{p} }
    = 0,
\end{align*}
and hence,
\begin{equation*}
  \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{t} \eta ]
= \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} {\eta }_{s} ] ds
= \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{s} {\eta }_{t} ] ds
= \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \frac{ {\eta }_{t} }{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds \bigg].
\end{equation*}
Since
\begin{equation*}
  \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \bigg| \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds \bigg|^{p} \bigg]
\le \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{s} |^{p} ] ds
  \to \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}_{t} |^{p} ], \quad as \quad \varepsilon \downarrow 0,
\end{equation*}
one can conclude that there exists a sufficiently small ${\delta }_{t} > 0$ such that $\frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}_{s} ] ds$ is uniformly integrable in $\varepsilon \in ( 0, {\delta }_{t} )$,
which implies that
\begin{align*}
  \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \bigg| \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \frac{ {\eta }_{t} }{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds \bigg] \bigg|
& \le ( \esssup | \eta | ) \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \bigg| \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds \bigg| \bigg] \\
& = ( \esssup | \eta | ) \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \bigg| \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}_{s} ] ds \bigg| \bigg] 
  = 0.
\end{align*}
Therefore, $\mathbb{E} [ {Y}_{t} \eta ] = 0$, a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$ for any $\eta \in \mathcal{D}$, and hence, ${Y}_{t} = 0$, a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.
\end{proof}


\section{Sufficient and necessary conditions for non-trivial ONECs}
\label{sec: Sufficient and necessary condition for non-trivial ONEC}

Now, we state the characterization of the ONECs.
Notably, we obtain not only its sufficiency as \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} did but also its necessity.
In addition, \cite[Proposition 3.3]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} for necessity no longer holds for our problem due to the presence of the non-separable $( {X}^{u}_{T} )^{j-1} {\varphi }^{t,u}_{j}$ in the terminal condition of ${Y}^{t,u}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: maximum principle}
Suppose that $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ is non-trivial and satisfies Assumptions \ref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} and \ref{ass: uniform integrability condition}.
Then, $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if
\begin{equation}
{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} {B}_{t} + \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} {D}_{t} = 0, \quad {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \le 0, \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: equilibrium condition :eq}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Plugging the terminal conditions in \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} into the expansion given by \Cref{lem: spike perturbation}
and applying It\^o's rule, we obtain
\begin{align*}
  {J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
& = \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} + {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} ( {y}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta }_{T} )^{2} ] + o ( \varepsilon ) \\
& = \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \Big( ( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} {B}_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} {D}_{s} ) \zeta + {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} | {D}_{s} |^{2} {\zeta }^{2} \Big) ds \bigg] + o ( \varepsilon ).
\end{align*}
Since the functions $( B,D )$ are bounded and ${Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }$ is uniformly integrable and $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. continuous, we have
\begin{align}
& \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } | ( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} ) {B}_{s} | ds \bigg] 
\le ( \sup |B| ) \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ | {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} | ] ds, 
\label{eq: continuity of Y :eq} \\
& \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } | ( \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} ) {D}_{s} | ds \bigg] 
\le ( \sup |D| ) \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ | \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} | ] ds, 
\label{eq: continuity of calY :eq} \\ 
& \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } | ( {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} ) | | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \bigg]
\le ( \sup |D|^{2} ) \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ | {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} | ] ds = o ( \varepsilon ). \notag
\end{align}
Under \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity}, because of \cref{eq: polynomial expression of Y :eq}, there exists some constant $K > 0$ such that
\begin{align*}
  \mathbb{E} [ | {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} | ]
& \le {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } 
      \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} \mathbb{E} \Big[ | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} | \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{s} [ | {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} |^{2n} ]^{ \frac{k}{2n} } \Big] \\
& \le K {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } 
      \sum_{k=0}^{2n-1} \Big( \mathbb{E} \Big[ | \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ \frac{2n}{2n-k} } \Big] \Big)^{ \frac{2n-k}{2n} } 
                        \big( \mathbb{E} [ | {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} |^{2n} ] \big)^{ \frac{k}{2n} },
\end{align*}
which tends to $0$ as $s \downarrow t$.
This implies that $\lim_{s \downarrow t} \mathbb{E}_{t} [ | {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} | ] = 0$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., and hence, the right-hand side of \cref{eq: continuity of Y :eq} merely equals $o ( \varepsilon )$.
In terms of \cref{eq: continuity of calY :eq}, according to \cref{eq: polynomial expression of calY :eq} and \Cref{ass: uniform integrability condition}, we obtain
\begin{align*}
  \mathbb{E} [ | \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} - \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} | ]
& \le {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } 
      \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \mathbb{E} \Big[ | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} | | {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} | \Big] \\
& \le {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } 
      \sum_{k=1}^{2n-1} \Big( \mathbb{E} \Big[ | \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{t} - \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ \frac{2n}{2n-k} } \Big] \Big)^{ \frac{2n-k}{2n} } 
                        \Big( \mathbb{E} \Big[ \sup_{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ] } | {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ \frac{2n}{k} } \Big] \Big)^{ \frac{k}{2n} },
\end{align*}
which also tends to $0$ as $s \downarrow t$.
Thus, the right-hand side of \cref{eq: continuity of calY :eq} also equals $o ( \varepsilon )$.
Therefore,
\begin{equation*}
  {J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
= \zeta \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {B}_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {D}_{s} ] ds
+ {\zeta }^{2} {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} | {D}_{t} |^{2} \varepsilon + o ( \varepsilon ).
\end{equation*}

Now, it is easy to see the sufficiency of \cref{eq: equilibrium condition :eq} for ${J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} ) \le o ( \varepsilon )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., $t \in [ 0,T )$.
In terms of necessity, given the arbitrariness of $\zeta $, one obtains ${Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} \le 0$ and
\begin{equation}
\lim_{ \varepsilon \downarrow 0 } \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {B}_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {D}_{s} ] ds = 0, \quad a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ), ~ \mathbb{P}-a.s.
\label{eq: first-order equilibrium condition :eq}
\end{equation}
Notably, by Young's inequality and \Cref{ass: uniform integrability condition}, we have that
\begin{equation*}
\sup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } \mathbb{E} \big[ | {P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ 1 + \delta } \big]
\le \frac{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) }{2n} \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \sup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } | \bar{P}^{ k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ \frac{ 2n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2n - k ( 1 + \delta ) } } \bigg]
  + \frac{ k ( 1 + \delta ) }{2n} \sup_{ s \in [ 0, \tau ] } \mathbb{E} \big[ | {\xi }^{ 1,k, \bar{u} }_{s} |^{ \frac{2n}{k} } \big]
\end{equation*}
for any fixed $\tau \in ( 0,T )$, which implies that $\{ {Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {B}_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{ s, \bar{u} }_{s} {D}_{s} \}_{ s \in [ 0,T ) } \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) )$
due to \Cref{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process} with the expression \cref{eq: polynomial expression of calY :eq} and the boundedness of $( B,D )$.
By applying \Cref{lem: stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem},
from \cref{eq: first-order equilibrium condition :eq}, we immediately obtain ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} {B}_{t} + \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} {D}_{t} = 0$, a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., 
and hence complete this proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rem: equilibrium condition equivalent}
Suppose that $I \equiv 0$.
Plugging the following result:
\begin{equation*}
{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} = {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} ] = {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv }, \quad 
{Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} = {e}^{ 2 \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T} ], \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ \forall t \in [ 0,T ),
\end{equation*}
arising from \cref{eq: BSDEs solution :eq}, into \cref{eq: equilibrium condition :eq} yields the following equivalent condition for a non-trivial $\bar{u}$ being an ONEC:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} = - \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} } {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv }, \quad 
\gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} ] )^{j-2} \big] {\varphi }_{j}^{ t, \bar{u} } \ge 0, \quad 
\mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ].
\end{equation*}
On the other hand, we should note that the above equivalent condition cannot ensure that the ONEC $\bar{u}$ is necessarily non-trivial under general parameter settings.
For example, for the case
\begin{equation*}
{J}^{t} (u) = \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] - \frac{\gamma }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{2} \big] 
                                             - \mathbb{E}_{t} \big[ ( {X}^{u}_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{u}_{T} ] )^{4} \big]
\end{equation*}
included in \cite[Example 5.6]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, since the sufficient condition of an ONEC does not prohibit the left-hand side of (5.6) therein (see also \cref{eq: equilibrium inequality :eq} in this paper) from vanishing,
$B \equiv 0$ would result in a trivial ONEC.
\end{remark}

Even when all partial derivatives ${\varphi }_{j}$ are constant,
it is still challenging to verify the ansatz that ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s}$ is a polynomial of 
$( {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t}, {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s}, \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} ], \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{t} [ {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} ], \ldots, \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{t} [ ( {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} )^{2n-1} ] )$ in the spirit of \cite[Section 4]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012}.
This is due to the presence of the polynomial of $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\bar{u}}_{T} ]$ and the non-separable ${X}^{\bar{u}}_{T} \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\bar{u}}_{T} ]$ in the expression of ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{T}$.
As a consequence, the method in \cite[Section 4]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012} for proving the uniqueness of an ONEC might not be applicable to our problem in general.
Furthermore, when one attempts to establish the forward SDE for ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }$ by applying It\^o's rule to ${Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} = H ( t, {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t}, s, {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} )$, 
it is also difficult to characterize this random field $H$.
On the other hand, the equilibrium condition \cref{eq: equilibrium condition :eq} seems unlikely to be usable for deriving an ONEC because the explicit expression of $\mathcal{Y}^{t}$ remains unclear.
In the remainder of this section, we use the method of stochastic Feynman-Kac representation, namely, the backward stochastic partial differential equation (BSPDE), to characterize the ONEC.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: BSPDE characterization}
For a non-trivial $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$ satisfying Assumptions \ref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} and \ref{ass: uniform integrability condition}, we write
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
& {\beta }_{t} := \frac{1}{ {D}_{t} } {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } \Big( {I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} \Big), \\
& i.e., \quad \bar{u}_{t} = {\beta }_{t} {e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } - \frac{1}{ {D}_{t} } ( {I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {F}_{t} )
\end{aligned} \right.
\label{eq: ONEC candidate :eq}
\end{equation}
with $\beta \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$, and then introduce the martingale representations
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
& \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{0}^{T} {B}_{s} {\beta }_{s} ds \bigg] = \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \int_{0}^{T} {B}_{s} {\beta }_{s} ds \bigg] + \int_{0}^{t} \mathbb{M}_{s} d {W}_{s}, \\
& \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{0}^{T} ( {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathbb{M}_{s} ) {I}_{s} ds \bigg] = \mathbb{E}^{(1)} \bigg[ \int_{0}^{T} ( {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathbb{M}_{s} ) {I}_{s} ds \bigg] + \int_{0}^{t} \mathbb{M}^{*}_{s} d {W}^{(1)}_{s}, \\
\end{aligned} \right.
\label{eq: beta martingale representation :eq} 
\end{equation}
with $\mathbb{M}, \mathbb{M}^{*} \in \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n} ( \Omega ) )$.
Suppose that the following BSPDE (indexed by $i = 0,1,2$ and $j = 1,2, \ldots, 2n-1$)
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
- d {\Phi }^{i,j} ( t,x ) & = \bigg\{ \frac{1}{2} {\Phi }^{i,j}_{xx} ( t,x ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} )^{2} 
                                    + {\Psi }^{i,j}_{x} ( t,x ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} )
                                    + i {\Phi }^{i,j}_{x} ( t,x ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} ) {I}_{t} \bigg\} dt \\
                          & \quad - {\Psi }^{i,j} ( t,x ) ( d {W}_{t} - i {I}_{t} dt ), \\
    {\Phi }^{i,j} ( T,x ) & = {x}^{j},
\end{aligned} \right.
\label{eq: BSPDE auxiliary :eq}
\end{equation}
admits a solution $( {\Phi }^{i,j}, {\Psi }^{i,j} ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/j} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) ) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/j} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) )$.
Then, $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if
\begin{align*}
  \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} }
& = ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} ) 
    \bigg( \gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) {\Phi }^{1,j-2} ( t,0 ) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big) \bigg) \\
& \quad + \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} } \bigg( \gamma {\Phi }^{1,1} ( t,0 )
                                           + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( {\Phi }^{1,j-1} ( t,0 ) - {\Phi }^{0,j-1} ( t,0 ) \Big) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big) \bigg) \\
& \quad + \gamma \mathbb{M}^{*}_{t}
        + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j {\Psi }^{1,j-1} ( t,0 ) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big), \\
0 & \le \gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) {\Phi }^{2,j-2} ( t,0 ) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big), \quad a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ], ~ \mathbb{P}-a.s.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the sake of brevity, hereafter we omit the statements ``a.e. $t \in [ 0,T ]$'' and ``$\mathbb{P}$-a.s.'' unless otherwise mentioned, because this rigorous modification merely leads to a few subtle questions during calculations.
Let us introduce the process ${X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }$ based on the following linear controlled SDE:
\begin{align*}
d {X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }_{s} & = \Big( {A}_{s} {X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }_{s} - \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } {I}_{s} ( {X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }_{s} - {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} ) + {B}_{s} \bar{u}_{s} + {C}_{s} \Big) ds \\
                           & \quad + ( {I}_{s} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} + {D}_{s} \bar{u}_{s} + {F}_{s} ) d {W}_{s}, \quad s \in [ t,T ], \quad {X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }_{t} = x.  
\end{align*}
Notably, ${X}^{ \bar{u} } = {X}^{ 0, {x}_{0}, \bar{u} }$, and it follows from \cref{eq: ONEC candidate :eq} and \cref{eq: beta martingale representation :eq} that
\begin{align}
{X}^{ t,x, \bar{u} }_{T} & = x {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } 
                           + \int_{t}^{T} {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } \Big( {C}_{s} - \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } {F}_{s} \Big) ds \notag \\
                   & \quad + \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{T} {B}_{s} {\beta }_{s} ds \bigg]
                           + \int_{t}^{T} ( {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathbb{M}_{s} ) d {W}_{s}.
\label{eq: auxiliary process X :eq}
\end{align}
Then, we consider ${M}^{i,j,m} ( t,x ) := \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( {X}^{ t, x, \bar{u} }_{T} - m )^{j} ]$ for $i=0,1,2$, $j = 0, 1, \ldots, 2n$ and $m \in \mathbb{R}$,
which enables a semi-martingale decomposition due to \cref{eq: auxiliary process X :eq}.
In fact, ${M}^{i,j,m}$ has the following stochastic Feynman-Kac representation:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
- d {M}^{i,j,m} ( t,x ) & = \bigg\{ \frac{1}{2} {M}^{i,j,m}_{xx} ( t,x ) ( {I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} )^{2} + \mathcal{M}^{i,j,m}_{x} ( t,x ) ( {I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} ) \\
                        & \qquad  + {M}^{i,j,m}_{x} ( t,x ) \Big( {A}_{t} x - \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} } {I}_{t} ( x - {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} ) + {B}_{t} \bar{u} + {C}_{t} 
                                                                + i {I}_{t} ( {I}_{t} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{t} + {D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} ) \Big) \bigg\} dt \\
                        & \quad - \mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} ( t,x ) ( d {W}_{t} - i {I}_{t} dt ), \\
    {M}^{i,j,m} ( T,x ) & = ( x - m )^{j}.
\end{aligned} \right.
\end{equation*}
Interested readers can refer to \cite{Ma-Yong-1997,Yong-Zhou-1999} for the related theories.
Notably, as the pair $( {M}^{i,j,m}, \mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/j} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) ) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/j} ( \Omega; {C}^{2} ( \mathbb{R} ) ) )$
has been given by a conditional expectation of a polynomial and its semi-martingale decomposition,
one can get rid of the questions about the existence, uniqueness and a priori estimates of the solution for the corresponding BSPDE.
In particular, for $i=0,1$, $\mathcal{M}^{i,1,m} ( t,x ) = \mathbb{M}_{t} + i \mathbb{M}^{*}_{t}$ arises from
\begin{align*}
{M}^{i,1,m} ( t,x ) & = x {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } 
                      + \int_{t}^{T} {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} ( {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} ) dv } \Big( {C}_{s} - \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } {F}_{s} \Big) ds - m \\
                    & \quad + \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{T} {B}_{s} {\beta }_{s} ds \bigg] 
                            + i \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{t}^{T} ( {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathbb{M}_{s} ) {I}_{s} ds \bigg]
\end{align*}
and \cref{eq: beta martingale representation :eq}.
Furthermore, by the It\^o-Kunita-Ventzel formula (see \cite[Theorem 1.5.3.2]{Jeanblanc-Yor-Chesney-2009}),
\begin{align*}
  d {M}^{i,j,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) 
& = \Big( {M}^{i,j,m}_{x} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) ( {I}_{s} {X}^{\bar{u}}_{s} + {D}_{s} \bar{u}_{s} + {F}_{s} ) + \mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) \Big) ( d {W}_{s} - i {I}_{s} ds ) \\
& = \Big( j {M}^{i,j-1,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) \Big) ( d {W}_{s} - i {I}_{s} ds ),
\end{align*}
where the last equality is due to ${M}^{i,j,m}_{x} ( t,x ) = j {M}^{i,j-1,m} ( t,x ) \exp ( \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} - \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} dv )$ from \cref{eq: auxiliary process X :eq} and the definition of ${M}^{i,j,m}$.
Hence, owing to \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} and \cref{eq: BSDEs solution :eq}, we have the following expression for $( {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s}, \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} )$:
\begin{align*}
    {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} 
& = 1 - \gamma {M}^{ 1,1, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) \\
& \quad - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( {M}^{ 1, j-1, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) - {M}^{ 0,j-1, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) \Big) \\
& \qquad \qquad \times {\varphi }_{j} \bigg( {M}^{ 0, 2, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ), \ldots, {M}^{ 0, 2n, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) \bigg), \\
    {e}^{ - \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{s} 
& = - \gamma ( {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathbb{M}_{s} + \mathbb{M}^{*}_{s} ) \\
& \quad - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( ( j - 1 ) {M}^{ 1,j-2, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} + \mathcal{M}^{ 1, j-1, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) \Big) \\
& \qquad \qquad \times {\varphi }_{j} \bigg( {M}^{ 0, 2, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ), \ldots, {M}^{ 0, 2n, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) } ( t, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{t} ) \bigg).
\end{align*}

On the other hand, applying the It\^o-Kunita-Ventzel formula to \cref{eq: BSPDE auxiliary :eq} and ${\Gamma }^{t,x}_{s} = x + \int_{t}^{s} ( {D}_{v} {\beta }_{v} + \mathbb{M}_{v} ) d {W}_{v}$ yields
${\Phi }^{i,j} ( t,x ) = \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( {\Gamma }^{t,x}_{T} )^{j} ]$.
By plugging this result along with ${\Phi }^{i,j}_{x} ( t,x ) = j {\Phi }^{i,j-1} ( t,x )$ and ${\Phi }^{i,j}_{xx} ( t,x ) = j (j-1) {\Phi }^{i,j-2} ( t,x )$ back into \cref{eq: BSPDE auxiliary :eq} and letting $x=0$, one obtains
\begin{align*}
    d {\Phi }^{i,j} ( t,0 )
& = - \bigg\{ \binom{j}{2} {\Phi }^{i,j-2} ( t,0 ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} )^{2}
            + i {\Phi }^{i,j-1} ( t,0 ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} ) {I}_{t} \\
& \qquad \quad + {\Psi }^{i,j}_{x} ( t,0 ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} ) \bigg\} dt
    + {\Psi }^{i,j} ( t,0 ) ( d {W}_{t} - i {I}_{t} dt ).
\end{align*}
Consequently, for $j = 1,2, \ldots, 2n-1$, by differentiating
\begin{equation*}
{M}^{i,j,m} ( t,x ) = \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} \big[ \big( {M}^{0,1,0} ( t,x ) - m + {\Gamma }^{t,0}_{T} \big)^{j} \big] 
                    = \sum_{k=0}^{j} \binom{j}{k} \big( {M}^{0,1,0} ( t,x ) - m \big)^{j-k} {\Phi }^{i,k} ( t,0 )
\end{equation*}
w.r.t. $t$ and taking the $d {W}_{t}$ terms, one obtains
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} ( t,x ) & = j \mathbb{M}_{t} {M}^{i,j-1,m} ( t,x ) + \sum_{k=1}^{j} \binom{j}{k} \big( {M}^{0,1,0} ( t,x ) - m \big)^{j-k} {\Psi }^{i,k} ( t,0 ), \\
\mathcal{M}^{ i,j, {M}^{0,1,0} ( t,x ) } ( t,x ) & = j \mathbb{M}_{t} {\Phi }^{i,j-1} ( t,0 ) + {\Psi }^{i,j} ( t,0 ).
\end{align*}
Therefore, it follows that
\begin{equation*}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
{e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } {Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} 
& = 1 - \gamma {\Phi }^{1,1} ( t,0 )
      - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( {\Phi }^{1,j-1} ( t,0 ) - {\Phi }^{0,j-1} ( t,0 ) \Big) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big), \\
{e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } \mathcal{Y}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} 
& = - \gamma ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} + \mathbb{M}^{*}_{t} ) \\
& \quad - \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j \Big( ( j - 1 ) {\Phi }^{1,j-2} ( t,0 ) ( {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t} + \mathbb{M}_{t} ) + {\Psi }^{1,j-1} ( t,0 ) \Big) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big).
\end{aligned} \right.
\end{equation*}
In the same manner, one can obtain
\begin{equation*}
  {e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} ( 2 {A}_{v} + | {I}_{v} |^{2} ) dv } {Z}^{ t, \bar{u} }_{t} 
= \gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) {\Phi }^{2,j-2} ( t,0 ) {\varphi }_{j} \big( {\Phi }^{0,2} ( t,0 ), \ldots, {\Phi }^{0,2n} ( t,0 ) \big).
\end{equation*}
Owing to \Cref{thm: maximum principle}, our desired equivalence property immediately arises.
\end{proof}


\section{Closed-form solution}
\label{sec: Closed-form solution}

In this section, we show through a straightforward calculation process that
\cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is an ONEC for the more general objective functional \cref{eq: objective functional :eq} under \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} in the case with $I \equiv 0$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: non-trivial bounded deterministic control}
Suppose that $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T ) \cap \mathbb{L}^{\infty }_{loc} ( 0,T )$.
Then, \Cref{ass: uniform integrability condition} holds.
Furthermore, if $\bar{u}$ is non-trivial, then \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} holds.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For the sake of brevity, we consider the expression \cref{eq: ONEC candidate :eq} with ${\beta } \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T ) \cap \mathbb{L}^{\infty }_{loc} ( 0,T )$ therein.
According to \Cref{thm: BSPDE characterization} and its proof, $\mathbb{M} \equiv 0$ and $\mathbb{M}^{*} \equiv 0$ arise from \cref{eq: beta martingale representation :eq},
${\Psi }^{i,j} \equiv 0$ due to the vanishing diffusion of ${\Phi }^{i,j} ( t,x ) = \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( {\Gamma }^{t,x}_{T} )^{j} ] = \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} [ ( x + \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} d {W}_{s} )^{j} ]$,
and hence, $\mathcal{M}^{i,j,m} \equiv 0$ and $d {M}^{i,j,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) = j {M}^{i,j-1,m} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} ( d {W}_{s} - i {I}_{s} ds )$.
Given the martingale representation $\mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{t} [ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} )^{j} ] = \mathbb{E}^{(1)} [ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} )^{j} ] + \int_{0}^{t} {\xi }^{ 1,j, \bar{u} }_{s} d {W}^{(1)}_{s}$
with the uniqueness of ${\xi }^{ 1,j, \bar{u} } \in\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F}}^{2} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2n/j} ( \Omega ) )$,
we conclude that ${\xi }^{ 1,j, \bar{u} }_{s} = j {M}^{1,j-1,0} ( s, {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{s} ) {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} = j \mathbb{E}^{(1)}_{s} [ ( {X}^{ \bar{u} }_{T} )^{j-1} ] {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s}$.
For any $\tau \in ( 0,T )$, one can obtain $\sup_{ t \in [ 0, \tau ] } \mathbb{E} [ | {\xi }^{ 1,j, \bar{u} }_{t} |^{2n/j} ] < \infty $ due to Doob's maximal inequality and the boundedness of $( D, \beta )$.
Hence, \Cref{ass: uniform integrability condition} holds.

Furthermore, if $\bar{u}$ is non-trivial, then $\int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds > 0$ and ${\varphi }^{ t, \bar{u} }_{j} = {\varphi }_{j} ( \vec{\alpha } ( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds ) )$ for all $t \in [ 0,T )$.
\Cref{ass: local Lipschitz continuity} provides the boundedness of ${\varphi }_{j} ( \vec{\alpha } (y) )$ on $y \in [ \frac{1}{m}, \int_{0}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds ]$ for any sufficiently large $m$.
By the continuity of ${\varphi }^{ \cdot, \bar{u} }_{j}$, one can conclude that \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} holds.
\end{proof}

The following \Cref{thm: ONEC}, which is slightly stronger than \cite[Theorem 5.9]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, provides the sufficient and necessary condition for a non-trivial path-independent ONEC.
In fact, the proof is straightforward according to \Cref{thm: BSPDE characterization} with $\mathbb{M}, \mathbb{M}^{*}, {\Psi }^{i,j} \equiv 0$ and
\begin{equation*}
{\Phi }^{i,j} ( t,0 ) = \mathbb{E}^{(i)}_{t} \bigg[ \Big( \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} d {W}_{s} \Big)^{j} \bigg] 
                      = \sum_{k=0}^{j} \binom{j}{k} \Big( i \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds \Big)^{j-k} {\alpha }_{k} \bigg( \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg),
\end{equation*}
so we omit it for brevity.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: ONEC}
Suppose that $\bar{u}$ is non-trivial and given by \cref{eq: ONEC candidate :eq} with $\beta \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T ) \cap \mathbb{L}^{\infty }_{loc} ( 0,T )$.
Then, $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$,
\begin{align*}
  \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} }
& = {D}_{t} {\beta }_{t}
    \Bigg( \gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) {\varphi }_{j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \times \sum_{k=0}^{j-2} \binom{j-2}{k} \Big( \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds \Big)^{j-2-k} {\alpha }_{k} \bigg( \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \Bigg) \\
& \quad + \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ {D}_{t} } \bigg( \gamma \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds
                                           + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j {\varphi }_{j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \\
& \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad  \times \sum_{k=0}^{j-2} \binom{j-1}{k} \Big( \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds \Big)^{j-1-k} {\alpha }_{k} \bigg( \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \Bigg) \\
0 & \le \gamma + \sum_{j=2}^{2n} j (j-1) {\varphi }_{j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \\
& \qquad \quad   \times \sum_{k=0}^{j-2} \binom{j-2}{k} \Big( 2 \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds \Big)^{j-2-k} {\alpha }_{k} \bigg( \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg).
\end{align*}
In particular,
\begin{itemize}
\item in the case with all ${\varphi }_{j} \equiv 0$, $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$,
\begin{equation*}
\frac{ {B}_{t} }{ \gamma | {D}_{t} |^{2} } = {\beta }_{t} + \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ | {D}_{t} |^{2} } \int_{t}^{T} {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} {I}_{s} ds, \quad i.e., \quad
{\beta }_{t} = \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ \gamma | {D}_{t} |^{2} } {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} \frac{ {B}_{v} }{ {D}_{v} } {I}_{v} dv },
\end{equation*}
which implies that $\bar{u}$ is given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq};
\item in the case with $I \equiv 0$, $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$,
\begin{align}
  \frac{ {B}_{t} }{ | {D}_{t} |^{2} } 
- {\beta }_{t} \Bigg( \gamma + \sum_{j=1}^{n} 2j (2j-1) {\alpha }_{2j-2} \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) 
                                              {\varphi }_{2j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \Bigg) = 0,
\label{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} \\
\gamma + \sum_{j=1}^{n} 2j (2j-1) {\alpha }_{2j-2} \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) {\varphi }_{2j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) \ge 0.
\label{eq: equilibrium inequality :eq}
\end{align}
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

In general, our higher-order moment problem does not necessarily admit a deterministic ONEC $\bar{u} \in \mathbb{L}^{2} ( 0,T ) \cap \mathbb{L}^{\infty }_{loc} ( 0,T )$.
Nevertheless, in the case with $I \equiv 0$, referring to \cite[Example 5.6]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} and the fact that
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{j=1}^{n} j (2j-1) {\alpha }_{2j-2} (y) {\varphi }_{2j} \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big)
= \sum_{j=1}^{n} \frac{d {\alpha }_{2j} (y) }{dy} {\varphi }_{2j} \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big) 
= \frac{d}{dy} \varphi \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big), \quad \forall y \in \mathbb{R}_{+},
\label{eq: derivative of varphi-alpha :eq}
\end{equation}
one can find a solution $\beta $ for \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} by solving the following algebra equations indexed by $t \in [ 0,T )$:
\begin{equation*}
  \int_{t}^{T} \Big| \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } \Big|^{2} ds 
= \int_{0}^{ \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds } \Big( \gamma + 2 \frac{d}{dy} \varphi \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big) \Big)^{2} dy,
\end{equation*}
if $\frac{d}{dy} \varphi ( \vec{\alpha } (y) )$ is square-integrable on some interval $( 0, \rho ]$ with
\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{T} \Big| \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } \Big|^{2} ds \le \int_{0}^{\rho } \Big( \gamma + 2 \frac{d}{dy} \varphi \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big) \Big)^{2} dy < \infty.
\end{equation*}
Notably, this square-integrability condition for $\frac{d}{dy} \varphi ( \vec{\alpha } (y) )$ seems much more natural than that employed in \cite[Theorem 5.4]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025};
and this algorithm differs from the backward iteration method for differential/integral equations. 
It is supposed to find each $\int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds$ at first, 
and then substitute these integral results into the complicated summation on the left-hand side of \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} to produce a simple linear equation of each ${\beta }_{t}$.

Below, we state the sufficiency and necessity results related to the homogeneity condition that we have emphasized at the end of \Cref{sec: Problem formulation}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: MV solution is ONEC}
If \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} holds and $I \equiv 0$, then $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is an ONEC for the objective functionals given by \cref{eq: objective functional :eq}.
Conversely, if $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is an ONEC for \cref{eq: objective functional :eq}, then
\begin{equation*}
\frac{d}{dy} \varphi \big( \vec{\alpha } (y) \big) = 0, \quad \forall y \in \bigg( 0, \frac{1}{ {\gamma }^{2} } \int_{0}^{T} \Big| \frac{ {B}_{s} }{ {D}_{s} } \Big|^{2} ds \bigg).
\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Owing to the fact that $\int_{\tau }^{T} | {B}_{s} | ds > 0$ for every $\tau \in [ 0,T )$, $\bar{u}$ is non-trivial.
Since \Cref{thm: ONEC} has provided the sufficiency and necessity of \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} and \cref{eq: equilibrium inequality :eq} for \cref{eq: ONEC candidate :eq} giving a non-trivial ONEC,
in conjunction with a comparison between \cref{eq: ONEC candidate :eq} and \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq},
it suffices to show that ${\beta }_{t} = {\gamma }^{-1} {B}_{t} | {D}_{t} |^{-2}$, or equivalently,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{j=1}^{n} j (2j-1) {\alpha }_{2j-2} \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) {\varphi }_{2j} \bigg( \vec{\alpha } \Big( \int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds \Big) \bigg) = 0, \quad a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: vanishing derivative :eq}
\end{equation}
Under \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition}, owing to \cref{eq: derivative of varphi-alpha :eq}, the proof for sufficiency is straightforward.

In terms of necessity, due to \Cref{thm: ONEC}, $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is an ONEC for \cref{eq: objective functional :eq}
only if \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} admits the solution ${\beta }_{t} = {\gamma }^{-1} {B}_{t} | {D}_{t} |^{-2}$ for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$.
This necessity condition implies that \cref{eq: vanishing derivative :eq} holds for a.e. $t \in \{ s \in [ 0,T ): | {B}_{s} | > 0 \}$.
As $\int_{t}^{T} | {D}_{s} {\beta }_{s} |^{2} ds = {\gamma }^{-2} \int_{t}^{T} | {B}_{s} |^{2} | {D}_{s} |^{-2} ds$ is absolutely continuous and decreasing in $t$,
\cref{eq: vanishing derivative :eq} must hold for every $t \in [ 0,T )$, and then our desired necessity result follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{rem: trivial ONEC}
If we have not imposed the condition that $\int_{\tau }^{T} | {B}_{s} | ds > 0$ for every $\tau \in [ 0,T )$, \Cref{thm: MV solution is ONEC} is still true, provided the major promise that $| \varphi ( \vec{\alpha } (0) ) | < \infty $ is well-defined.
Thus, $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is a trivial ONEC in the case with $I \equiv 0$ and ${\tau }_{0} := \inf \{ t \in [ 0,T ]: \int_{t}^{T} | {B}_{s} | ds = 0 \} < T$,
since \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} with \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} implies that ${D}_{t} \bar{u}_{t} + {F}_{t} = 0$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., a.e. $t \in [ {\tau }_{0}, T ]$.

To provide a rigorous justification for this extension, we should consider the perturbation argument (see \Cref{lem: spike perturbation}) and the BSDEs \cref{eq: BSDEs :eq} for $t \in [ 0, {\tau }_{0} )$.
In addition, \Cref{ass: uniform integrabiity condition with continuity} should be replaced, with a slight abuse of notation, by
${\varphi }_{j}^{ \cdot, \bar{u} } \in {C}_{\mathbb{F},loc} ( 0, {\tau }_{0}; \mathbb{L}^{ \frac{ 2 n ( 1 + \delta ) }{ 2 n - ( j - 1 ) ( 1 + \delta ) } } ( \Omega ) )$ for all $j$.
Consequently, \Cref{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process} provides the uniqueness of the diagonal process quadruplet in
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } \big( 0, {\tau }_{0}; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} \big( 0, {\tau }_{0}; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times 
\mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{\infty } \big( 0, {\tau }_{0}; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big) \times \mathbb{L}_{\mathbb{F},loc}^{2} \big( 0, {\tau }_{0}; \mathbb{L}^{ 1 + \delta } ( \Omega ) \big).
\end{equation*}
On the other hand, as ${B}_{s} = 0$ for a.e. $s \in [ {\tau }_{0}, T )$ and \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition} holds, it follows from \cref{eq: perturbation argument for trivial ONEC :eq} that
\begin{equation*}
{J}^{t} ( \bar{u}^{ t, \varepsilon, \zeta } ) - {J}^{t} ( \bar{u} )
= - \frac{\gamma }{2} {\zeta }^{2} \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } {e}^{ 2 \int_{s}^{T} {A}_{v} dv } | {D}_{s} |^{2} ds \le 0, \quad \forall t \in [ {\tau }_{0}, T ).
\end{equation*}
Therefore, mirroring the proof of \Cref{thm: maximum principle}, we can show that $\bar{u}$ is an ONEC if and only if the equilibrium condition \cref{eq: equilibrium condition :eq} $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. holds for a.e. $t \in [ 0, {\tau }_{0} )$.
In view of the proofs of \Cref{lem: integrabiity and uniqueness of diagonal process} and \Cref{thm: ONEC},
one can find that the abovementioned equilibrium condition is equivalent to \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} and \cref{eq: equilibrium inequality :eq} for a.e., $t \in [ 0, {\tau }_{0} )$.
Notably, $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} satisfies \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} and \cref{eq: equilibrium inequality :eq} for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$ under \Cref{ass: homogeneity condition},
and hence, it is an ONEC.
Conversely, owing to the previous justification, $\bar{u}$ given by \cref{eq: MV time-consistent solution :eq} is an ONEC only if \cref{eq: equilibrium equation :eq} for a.e. $t \in [ 0, {\tau }_{0} )$.
By mirroring the proof of \Cref{thm: MV solution is ONEC}, the necessity immediately arises.
\end{remark}


\section{Concluding remarks}
\label{sec: Concluding remark}

We studied a time-inconsistent stochastic control problem with higher-order central moments and a linear controlled SDE.
We relaxed the assumptions employed in the existing studies, including the local Lipschitz continuity and integrability conditions, 
and provided not only the sufficiency of the equilibrium condition for an ONEC but also its necessity.
On the one hand, to arrive at the equilibrium condition, we studied a flow of linear BSDEs indexed by different initial epochs.
Exploiting the theory of BSDEs with $\mathbb{L}^{p}$-integrable data, in conjunction with the linearity that indeed arises from the controlled SDE,,
we demonstrated the integrability of the diagonal processes generated by the abovementioned flow of BSDEs.
On the other hand, we further extended the stochastic Lebesgue differentiation theorem for the necessity of the equilibrium condition.
In particular, we considered the case in which the diffusion of the controlled SDE does not explicitly rely on the state variable, 
and then found that a deterministic function is an ONEC if and only if it satisfies some integral equation and inequality.
Moreover, we provided the sufficiency and ``necessity'' of the homogeneity condition for the mean-variance equilibrium strategy being an ONEC for the higher-order moment problem.

Some challenging problems remain to be addressed in future research, such as further extending our theory for fairly general controlled SDEs or more general objective functionals that even rely on some regular conditional laws.
The most important issue is the existence and uniqueness of equilibrium.
In general, the abovementioned integral equation that produces ONECs is not linear, 
and its boundedness and Lipschitz continuity are not necessarily valid under our relaxed assumptions (see also \cite[Theorem 5.4]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} for comparison).
Due to the presence of non-separable random variables in the data of each BSDE, one cannot mirror the method described in \cite[Section 4]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} to show the uniqueness of the feedback form of equilibrium control.

