	\subsection{Performance of Zero Intermediate Reward}\label{subsec:cu_rl_zero_reward}
	We consider a different reward function defined in \eqref{reward_zero}, which we call the zero intermediate reward, to test whether the CU-RL method can still perform well without intermediate information about the hedging error before maturity. We look into the performance of four unified CU-RL models, which are for all short-term near-the-money call options with $T \in [5, 30]$ and $K/S_{0} \in [0.9, 1.1]$, all short-term near-the-money put options with $T \in [5, 30]$ and $K/S_{0} \in [0.9, 1.1]$,
	all call options, and all put options, 
	 respectively. 

	\begin{equation}
		\label{reward_zero}
		R_{t+1}=
		\begin{cases}
			0, & t=0, \cdots, T-2, \\
			-\lambda_{1}\left[\omega + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \max (-W_{t+1}-\omega, 0)\right] + \lambda_{2} W_{t+1}, & t=T-1.
		\end{cases} 
	\end{equation} 

	For the short-term near-the-money call/put options, we utilize the same value of hyperparameters as those in the experiments with the asymmetric reward in \eqref{R_relu}, which is demonstrated in the previous sections. 
