	\subsection{Performance of Reward Divided by Initial Margin}\label{subsec:cu_rl_divided_by_margin_reward}
	If we are more concerned about the relative value of return, the reward function is correspondingly defined as a ratio. We consider a naive case, in which the reward is divided by the initial margin. At first, we define the reward as \eqref{reward_asym_margin}. 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{reward_asym_margin}
		R_{t+1}=
		\begin{cases}
			-\frac{|W_{t+1}|}{M_{0}} \cdot \mathbb{I}_{ \{ W_{t+1}<0 \} }, & t=0, \cdots, T-2, \\
			-\lambda_{1}\left[\omega + \frac{1}{1-\alpha} \max (-\frac{W_{t+1}}{M_{0}}-\omega, 0)\right] + \lambda_{2}\frac{W_{t+1}}{M_{0}}, & t=T-1,
		\end{cases} 
	\end{equation} 
	where $M_{0}$ is the initial value in the margin account. The computation of the initial margin obeys the following rule given by CBOE.

	\begin{itemize}
		\item Rule for initial margin:

