\begin{abstract}



A Coulomb-excitation study of the high-lying first excited state at 4.439 MeV in the nucleus $^{12}$C has been carried out using the $^{208}$Pb($^{12}$C,$^{12}$C$^*$)$^{208}$Pb$^*$ reaction at 56 MeV
and the {\sc Q3D} magnetic spectrograph at the Maier-Leibnitz Laboratorium in Munich.
High-statistics achieved with an average beam intensity of approximately 10$^{11}$ ions/s together with state-of-the-art {\it ab initio} calculations of the nuclear dipole polarizability
permitted the accurate determination of the spectroscopic quadrupole moment, $Q_{_S}(2_{_1}^+) = +0.076(30)$~eb, in agreement with previous measurements.
% , confirming a large oblate deformation.
% for the high-lying first excited state at 4.439 MeV in $^{12}$C
%, which underestimates the extent of the quadrupole deformation.
% This result adopts state-of-the-art {\it ab initio} calculations of the competing nuclear dipole polarizability and is supported by similar high-statistics measurements.
Combined with previous work, a weighted average of $Q_{_S}(2_{_1}^+) = +0.090(14)$ eb is determined, which includes the re-analysis of a similar experiment by Vermeer and collaborators,
$Q_{_S}(2_{_1}^+) = +0.103(20)$~eb.
Such a large oblate deformation challenges modern nuclear theory and emphasizes
% We further investigate the competing $E1$ polarization effect using a phenomenological interaction and compared with {\it ab initio} calculations, emphasizing
% the need for dedicated Coulomb-excitation measurements of the dipole polarizability in even-even nuclei, and
the  need of $\alpha$ clustering and associated triaxiality effects for full convergence of $E2$ collective properties.
%which underestimate by about a half the extent of deformation.
\end{abstract}



