An $n \times n$ strictly upper triangular matrix $M$ is said to have an $\alpha_1 (k,l)$-$\alpha_2 (l-1, m)$-formation (or simply $\alpha_1$-$\alpha_2$-formation) if $M=M_1+M_2-M_3$, where $M_1$ is a matrix of the $\alpha_1 (k,l)$-formation, $M_2$ is a matrix of the $\alpha_2(l-1, m)$-formation, and $M_3$ is the matrix such that $M_3(l-1, l)=2$ and the other entries are 0. 
(See Figure \ref{fig-cc-ex}.)
\end{definition}

