\section{Introduction}
%Research suggests that improving students' sense of belonging, defined by the feeling of being valued, accepted, and a feeling like a legitimate member of their chosen scientific discipline can reduce attrition rates \cite{dobbins2020time, Lewis2016, walton2007question} [Dobbins 2020, Lewis 2016, Walton and Cohen 2007, proceedings]. Development of student disciplinary identity and motivational beliefs can potentially increase student resilience within a chosen STEM major \cite{Hazari2010} [Hazari 2010, proceedings]. Additionally, participating in co-curricular experiences that develop abilities beyond the focus of classroom and laboratory studies, including informal communication, teamwork, and creativity, have been identified by the American Physics Society (APS) and American Association of Physics Teachers (AAPT) as a high priority for physics programs to prepare students for 21st century careers \cite{Heron2016} [Heron 2016, Smith 2021, proposal]. These studies clearly indicate that impactful, engaging courses must be combined with experiences outside the classroom to better provide undergraduate physics students with the opportunity to see themselves as contributing members of the physics community. 

