\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Given a map \(\hat{A}: X \to \mathcal{F}_0\), we define $S\hat{A} \colon SX \to S\mathcal{F}_0$ as the map induced by the following mapping:
\begin{align*}
&X \times I \to \mathcal{F}_1, &(x, t)\mapsto (\hat{A}_x, t).
\end{align*}
Notice that, by definition, the equality \(\phi \circ S\hat{A} = AS(\hat{A})\) holds. Additionally, the suspension isomorphism in singular cohomology is essentially a connecting homomorphism. Therefore, for each integer \( n \in \mathbb{Z} \), the claim follows from the calculations below.
\begin{align*}
&\delta(\operatorname{ch}_n(\hat{A}))=\delta(\hat{A}^{*}\operatorname{ch}_n^{univ})=(SA)^{*}\delta(\operatorname{ch}_n^{univ})=(SA)^{*}\phi^*(\operatorname{ch}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}^{univ})\\
&=AS(\hat{A})^{*}\operatorname{ch}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}^{univ}
=\operatorname{ch}_{n+\frac{1}{2}}(AS(\hat{A})).
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

