\subsection{Model Validation}
\label{sec:validation}

The cross-validation experiments (Section~\ref{sec:cv_results}) confirmed the capacity of our SBI framework to recover key micro-level parameters from aggregate ASFRs and ASUFR data. To further scrutinize the model's performance, we conduct two additional forms of validation. The first, posterior predictive checks, assesses the model's ability to replicate the aggregate data patterns used for inference, thus evaluating its internal consistency and goodness-of-fit. The second, and more demanding, validation stage examines the model's capacity to predict out-of-sample individual-level characteristics of the observed cohorts, such as distributions of desired family size, age at first sexual intercourse, and birth interval durations, which were not used during the parameter estimation process. 

For both validation exercises, we focus on parameter posteriors obtained under Scenario 2 (ASFRs + ASUFR with weakly informative priors). This scenario is chosen for its parsimonious data requirements (relying only on the age-specific share of unplanned births alongside total ASFRs) while having demonstrated strong parameter recovery in the cross-validation exercises.

\subsubsection{Posterior Predictive Checks for Aggregate Rates}
\label{sec:ppc}

To perform posterior predictive checks, we drew 5,000 samples from the joint posterior distribution of the model parameters for each of the four countries (United States, Colombia, Dominican Republic, and Peru). For each parameter sample, we simulated a full cohort of individual reproductive histories using our model and then recomputed the aggregate ASFRs and ASUFRs series.

Figure~\ref{fig:ppc_plots} presents the empirically observed ASFRs and ASUFRs curves against their corresponding posterior predictive distributions. In these plots, observed total ASFRs are marked by purple open circles and observed ASUFRs by purple open triangles. The red open circles and triangles indicate the mean of the 5,000 simulated ASFRs and ASUFRs schedules, respectively, while the shaded red areas depict the 97.5\% posterior predictive intervals. 

Across all four diverse demographic contexts, the model demonstrates a strong capacity to replicate the observed data. The posterior predictive means for both ASFRs and ASUFR closely track the empirical curves, capturing key features such as the location and height of peak fertility, the age pattern of decline, and the distinct levels and shapes of unplanned fertility. For instance, the distinct, lower peak of the ASUFRs in the US is well reproduced. For Colombia (Figure~\ref{fig:ppc_plots}b), the Dominican Republic (Figure~\ref{fig:ppc_plots}c), and Peru (Figure~\ref{fig:ppc_plots}d), characterized by higher overall fertility, the model successfully replicates not only the higher ASFRs levels but also the specific age gradients of both total and unplanned births; in Colombia and Peru, however, there is a discernible tendency for the predictive mean to underestimate the peak of the ASFRs, though the observed data remain well within the prediction intervals. Notably, across all countries, almost all empirically observed points for both ASFRs and ASUFRs fall well within their respective 97.5\% posterior predictive intervals, indicating that the model, conditioned on the inferred parameters, generates aggregate outcomes highly consistent with the observed data.

These results confirm that our framework, even with the relatively minimal data inputs of Scenario 2, can consistently and accurately reproduce the population-level fertility and unplanned fertility schedules it was intended to explain. This provides strong confidence in the internal validity of the estimated parameters before assessing their ability to predict out-of-sample, individual-level outcomes.

